<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · Egypt, North Africa and Sudan</p><h1 class="st-title">Hospitaller Tripoli</h1><p class="st-meta">1530 CE – 1551 CE · capital Tripoli</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Tripoli under the Knights of St John, a reluctant outpost they held for two decades before losing it to the Ottoman fleet in 1551.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1530 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> granted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malta">Malta</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/spanish-tripoli">Spanish Tripoli</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knights-hospitaller">Knights Hospitaller</a>, homeless since losing <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/rhodes">Rhodes</a> in 1522. The Order accepted <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-tripoli">Tripoli</a> unwillingly, as it was exposed and expensive, but took over the garrison under its own governors.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The knights ruled from the old castle, strengthening its walls and using the port as a base for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galley">galleys</a> against Muslim shipping. Their small force depended on supplies from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/hospitaller-malta">Hospitaller Malta</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>. Relations with the surrounding Arab population and with the corsair <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turgut-reis">Turgut Reis</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tajura">Tajura</a> were hostile.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In August 1551 an Ottoman fleet under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sinan-pasha-ottoman-admiral">Sinan Pasha</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turgut-reis">Turgut Reis</a> besieged <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-tripoli">Tripoli</a>. The governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaspard-de-vallier">Gaspard de Vallier</a> surrendered after a brief bombardment, and the city became <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/ottoman-tripolitania">Ottoman Tripolitania</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/hospitaller-tripoli.jpg" alt="Hospitaller Tripoli" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Zscout370 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Order_of_St._John_(various).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1530 CE – 1551 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Tripoli</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Philippe Villiers de L'Isle-Adam, Juan de Homedes, Gaspard de Vallier</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>French, Spanish, Italian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/libya">Libya</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Tripoli of the Knights of Malta</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1540&amp;focus=hospitaller-tripoli" aria-label="Hospitaller Tripoli on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5018 983 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Hospitaller Tripoli lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5338 1239L5338 1239M5338 1239L5366 1137M5338 1239L5338 1239"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5338 1239h0M5366 1137h0M5338 1239h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5338 1239h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5338 1239h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Tripoli. <a href="/atlas?year=1540&amp;focus=hospitaller-tripoli">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
